Basic strength depends upon availability of lone pairs. Greater the resonance of lone pairs lesser the basic strength. In $(\mathrm{II})\text{and}(\mathrm{III})$ lone pair is localised. $(\mathrm{III})$ is more basic due to $+ I$ effect of carbon chain whereas in $\mathrm{II}$, negative mesomeric effect is operating. In $(I)\text{and}(\mathrm{IV})$ lone pair is delocalised, so their basic strength is less that former two amines. If $(\mathrm{IV})$ donates its lone pair then its aromaticity will get lost therefore, its basic strength is less than that of $I$.

Overall order of basic strength is $\left(\mathrm{III}\right) > \left(\mathrm{II}\right) > \left(I\right) > \left(\mathrm{IV}\right)$

